  "worked_solution": "The function is strictly increasing where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $f(x)$ using the product rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{2}-8){e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}=({x}^{2}+2x-8){e}^{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+2x-8){e}^{x}>0.\n$$\n\nThe exponential function is always positive, so this reduces to\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2x-8>0.\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x+4)>0.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=(x-2)(x+4)$ has roots at $x=2$ and $x=-4,$ as shown below:\n\n![](@asset-0@)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the intervals $x<-4$ and $x>2.$"
}